# DART Data Assimilation — Experiment Documentation
## Overview

This documentation covers data assimilation experiments conducted using
**DART (Data Assimilation Research Testbed)** — an open-source ensemble
DA framework developed by NCAR/UCAR.

Three models were studied in order of increasing complexity:

| Model | Variables | Dimensions | Purpose |
|-------|-----------|------------|---------|
| [Lorenz 63](lorenz63.md) | 3 (x, y, z) | 0D — 3 equations | Learn DA basics |
| [Lorenz 96](lorenz96.md) | 40 (x1–x40) | 1D — latitude circle | Test inflation and localization |
| [B-grid Solo](bgrid.md) | 28,200 (Ps, T, u, v) | 3D — global atmosphere | Real atmospheric DA |

---

## What is Data Assimilation?

Data assimilation (DA) mathematically combines two imperfect sources of
information to produce the best possible estimate of the true atmospheric state:

1. **A computer model** — simulates the atmosphere but drifts from reality
2. **Observations** — real measurements but sparse and noisy

DART uses the **Ensemble Kalman Filter (EnKF)** — running many slightly
different model forecasts (an ensemble) and using their spread to estimate
uncertainty, then correcting them toward observations.

