## Experiment 1 — Observation Error Variance

### Purpose

Determine how changing observation error variance **R** affects
assimilation performance. Four values of R were tested.

### Key Concept

- **Large R** → DART trusts observations less → weaker corrections
- **Small R** → DART trusts observations more → stronger corrections

### Results

| R | Prior RMSE | Posterior RMSE |
|---|------------|----------------|
| 8.0 | 0.1780 | 0.1730 | 2.8% |
| 2.0 | 0.0981 | 0.0943 | 3.9% |
| 0.5 | 0.0535 | 0.0504 | 5.8% |
| 0.2 | 0.0339 | 0.0310 | **8.6% ✓ Best** |

### Conclusion

- Smaller observation error variance produced lower RMSE across all cases
- Posterior RMSE was always smaller than prior RMSE — assimilation always helped
- As R decreased from 8.0 to 0.2 the prior RMSE dropped by **81%**
- The filter gave more weight to observations when R was small — pulling
  the ensemble closer to truth

---

## Experiment 2 — Observation Frequency

### Purpose

Study how the number of observations affects forecast error growth
and assimilation performance.

### Setup

Two observation network sizes were tested:

| Setup | Observations | Description |
|-------|-------------|-------------|
| Sparse | 50 observations | Few measurements |
| Dense | 200 observations | More frequent updates |

### Key Findings

- **Fewer observations** caused larger forecast errors — less information
  to constrain the ensemble
- **More frequent observations** reduced error growth — ensemble tracked
  truth more closely
- Frequent updates prevented the chaotic model from drifting too far
  from the true state between corrections

---

## Evaluation Metrics

### RMSE (Root Mean Square Error)

Measures the difference between the ensemble mean and the true state.
**Lower RMSE = better assimilation performance.**

```
RMSE = sqrt( mean( (ensemble_mean - truth)^2 ) )
```

Computed separately for:
- **Prior RMSE** — before assimilating observations
- **Posterior RMSE** — after assimilating observations

### Ensemble Spread

Measures the uncertainty in the ensemble forecast — how spread out
the 20 members are around their mean. A well calibrated ensemble has:

```
Spread ≈ RMSE   (Spread/RMSE ratio close to 1.0)
```

---

## Overall Conclusions

| Finding | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| Observation error effect | Smaller R → better RMSE. R=0.2 gave 81% lower prior RMSE than R=8.0 |
| Observation frequency effect | More observations → better error control and truth tracking |
| Posterior always better | In all experiments posterior RMSE < prior RMSE — DA always helped |
| Chaotic sensitivity | Lorenz 63's chaos makes observation quality critical — small R matters a lot |

> **Key message:** In chaotic systems like Lorenz 63, the quality and
> frequency of observations strongly controls how well data assimilation
> can track the true state. This motivates careful observation network
> design in real atmospheric DA systems.

