## What is B-grid Solo?

The B-grid Solo model is a **simplified 3D atmospheric model** based on
GFDL's B-grid dynamical core. It is the next step up in complexity from
Lorenz 96 — bridging the gap between toy models and full weather models.

| Feature | Lorenz 96 | B-grid Solo |
|---------|-----------|-------------|
| Dimensions | 1D circle | 3D atmosphere |
| Variables | 40 (one type) | 28,200 (four types) |
| Variable types | state only | Ps, T, u, v |
| Obs types | RAW_STATE | Radiosonde |
| Run time | Seconds | Minutes |

---

## Model Grid

```
Grid structure: 60 lon × 30 lat × 5 lev
                ↑         ↑        ↑
         longitude     latitude   vertical

Total variables: ~60 × 30 × 5 × 4 = 36,000
Actual model size: 28,200 (winds on VelJ=29 grid)
```

### Why VelJ = 29 (not 30)?

The B-grid uses **Arakawa B-grid staggering** — temperature and pressure
(T, Ps) sit at grid cell centers, while winds (u, v) sit at cell corners.
Corners of a 30-point latitude grid give 29 interior boundaries.

---

## State Variables

| Symbol | Name | Units | Dimensions | Notes |
|--------|------|-------|------------|-------|
| Ps | Surface Pressure | Pa | 2D (lat × lon) | Ground level only — no vertical dim |
| T | Temperature | K | 3D (lev × lat × lon) | Different at each level |
| u | Zonal Wind | m/s | 3D (lev × VelJ × lon) | East-west component |
| v | Meridional Wind | m/s | 3D (lev × VelJ × lon) | North-south component |

### Why is Ps 2D?

Surface pressure is the **total weight of the atmosphere** pressing down
on one point at the ground. It is measured at the surface only — there is
no such thing as "surface pressure at level 3." Hence no vertical dimension.

---

## Held-Suarez Forcing

The model uses **Held-Suarez forcing** — a standard benchmark for
atmospheric model testing (Held & Suarez, 1994).

- Maintains temperature gradient between tropics and poles
- Relaxes atmosphere toward an equilibrium temperature profile
- Creates realistic jet streams and weather-like patterns
- No moisture, clouds, or ocean — simplified but physically meaningful

Key parameters in `input.nml`:

```fortran
&hs_forcing_nml
   delh   = 60.    ! temperature gradient pole-to-equator
   t_zero = 315.   ! reference temperature (K)
   ka     = -40.   ! atmospheric relaxation timescale
/
```

---

## Key Parameters (input.nml)

| Parameter | Value | Namelist | Description |
|-----------|-------|----------|-------------|
| `nlon` | 60 | `&bgrid_cold_start_nml` | Longitude points |
| `nlat` | 30 | `&bgrid_cold_start_nml` | Latitude points |
| `nlev` | 5 | `&bgrid_cold_start_nml` | Vertical levels |
| `dt_atmos` | 3600 | `&model_nml` | 1 hour time step |
| `days` | 10 | `&model_nml` | Intended run length |
| `ens_size` | 20 | `&filter_nml` | Ensemble members |
| `inf_flavor` | 0 | `&filter_nml` | No inflation (baseline) |
| `cutoff` | 0.20 | `&assim_tools_nml` | ~1145 km localization radius |
| `perturb_from_single_instance` | .true. | `&filter_nml` | Create ensemble by perturbing |
| `perturbation_amplitude` | 0.2 | `&filter_nml` | Ensemble perturbation size |

